{"claim":"NFPA 72 requires emergency voice/alarm communications systems to achieve a Speech Transmission Index of at least 0.50 in areas where voice messaging is required.","source_title":"NFPA 72 National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code (2022 Edition), Chapter 18 Notification Appliances, §18.4.11 Intelligibility","source_url":null,"source_type":"technical_standard","is_primary":true,"page_or_section":"Chapter 18, §18.4.11.2 and Annex D (Speech Intelligibility)","supporting_excerpt":"Voice communication systems required to be intelligible shall be designed and installed to achieve a measured Speech Transmission Index of not less than 0.50 in each acoustically distinguishable space, measured in accordance with IEC 60268-16.","source_date":"2021-12-15","data_vintage":"2022 edition","airport_or_entity":"United States (national code)","units":"STI (dimensionless, 0–1)","denominator":"per acoustically distinguishable space","caveat":"Local jurisdictions adopt NFPA 72 by reference through building codes; the airport context packet (§8) confirms NFPA 72 is the primary technical authority for EVACS intelligibility in U.S. terminals. Excerpt is a faithful paraphrase of the code intent, not a verbatim quotation.","confidence":"high"}
{"claim":"14 CFR 139.325 requires each Part 139-certificated airport to maintain an Airport Emergency Plan that includes a communications network capable of prompt notification, but does not prescribe PA sound-pressure levels, STI thresholds, or paging cadence for terminals.","source_title":"14 CFR § 139.325 — Airport emergency plan","source_url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-G/part-139/subpart-D/section-139.325","source_type":"federal_regulation","is_primary":true,"page_or_section":"14 CFR §139.325","supporting_excerpt":"Each certificate holder must develop and maintain an airport emergency plan... The plan must contain instructions for response to... a communications network.","source_date":"2004-02-10","data_vintage":"current as of 2026","airport_or_entity":"United States (national regulation)","units":null,"denominator":null,"caveat":"Absence of specific acoustic/PA thresholds in Part 139 is analytically important: the airport operator must specify them through its own design manual; the regulator does not do it.","confidence":"high"}
