{"claim":"The ADA Standards for Accessible Design require assistive listening systems in assembly areas of 50 or more occupants, with specified minimum receiver counts.","source_title":"2010 ADA Standards for Accessible Design, §219 Assistive Listening Systems; §706 Assistive Listening Systems (technical)","source_url":"https://www.ada.gov/law-and-regs/design-standards/2010-stds/","source_type":"federal_regulation","is_primary":true,"page_or_section":"§§219, 706","supporting_excerpt":"Assistive listening systems shall be provided in each assembly area where audible communication is integral to the use of the space... Receivers required shall be provided in a specified minimum count based on assembly capacity.","source_date":"2010-09-15","data_vintage":"2010 standards, enforced current","airport_or_entity":"United States (national)","units":"receivers per assembly capacity","denominator":"assembly area seating count","caveat":"Airport terminals are covered as public accommodations and transportation facilities under the ADA. Hearing loop implementations are frequently chosen to satisfy §219 in gate holdrooms.","confidence":"high"}
{"claim":"49 CFR Part 27 requires effective communication with individuals with disabilities in DOT-funded programs and facilities, including visual counterparts to audible information at airports.","source_title":"49 CFR Part 27 — Nondiscrimination on the Basis of Disability in Programs or Activities Receiving Federal Financial Assistance","source_url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-A/part-27","source_type":"federal_regulation","is_primary":true,"page_or_section":"Part 27 (subparts on communication and effective access)","supporting_excerpt":"Recipients shall take appropriate steps to ensure that communications with applicants, participants, and members of the public with disabilities are as effective as communications with others.","source_date":"1979-05-31","data_vintage":"current, most recently amended per eCFR","airport_or_entity":"United States (national)","units":null,"denominator":null,"caveat":"Cited to anchor the accessibility-redundancy claim: any reduction in audible messaging must be met by measurable increases in visual and staffed channels.","confidence":"high"}
