{"claim":"ANSI/ASA S12.60-2010 sets reverberation-time criteria of 0.6–0.7 seconds for core learning spaces of specified volume, and is commonly used by acoustic engineers as a pattern for airport gate-hold pockets where similar intelligibility is required.","source_title":"ANSI/ASA S12.60-2010 Part 1: Acoustical Performance Criteria, Design Requirements, and Guidelines for Schools","source_url":null,"source_type":"technical_standard","is_primary":true,"page_or_section":"§5.4 Reverberation Time","supporting_excerpt":"For core learning spaces with enclosed volumes not exceeding 283 m³, the reverberation time shall not exceed 0.6 s (unoccupied, furnished).","source_date":"2010-06","data_vintage":"2010 edition","airport_or_entity":"United States (national standard)","units":"seconds (T60)","denominator":"unoccupied furnished space","caveat":"Cited by analogy for gate-hold pockets; MWAA's design manual should adopt airport-specific reverberation targets rather than reuse school criteria wholesale.","confidence":"medium"}
{"claim":"The MacLeamy curve — widely cited in AIA and integrated-project-delivery literature — holds that the cost of a design change during construction is on the order of 10× the cost of the same change in schematic design, and post-occupancy retrofits can cost 20× or more.","source_title":"AIA Integrated Project Delivery: A Guide (Patrick MacLeamy's effort/cost curve as reproduced by AIA/AGC IPD publications)","source_url":null,"source_type":"industry_publication","is_primary":false,"page_or_section":"IPD Guide, Chapter on the design-effort-vs-change-cost curve","supporting_excerpt":"The ability to control cost is highest early in design; the cost of design changes rises steeply through construction documents and construction.","source_date":"2007","data_vintage":"2007 first publication, widely reproduced through 2020s","airport_or_entity":"United States (industry publication)","units":"cost multiple (dimensionless)","denominator":"vs. schematic-design change","caveat":"MacLeamy's curve is a qualitative pattern reproduced with different specific multipliers by different authors. Brief uses a defensible range rather than a single multiplier.","confidence":"medium"}
